---
title: Quiche Lorraine
servings: 8
tags: [pork, make-ahead]
cuisine: french
time: 4 hours 45 minutes
source: Quiche Lorraine by Leah Colins, Serious Eats, https://www.seriouseats.com/quiche-lorraine-recipe-8715255
nutrition:
  calories: 522 kcal
---

== Crust ==

Make one single crust's worth of the buttery pie crust (baking/pies-and-tarts/buttery-pie-crust) and shape it into a 5-inch disc by rolling its edge on the counter. Wrap and chill ~{30%minutes}, until firm.

Roll it on a well-floured counter into a 12-inch round; if it cracks, let it sit ~{5%minutes} first. Roll it loosely around a #rolling pin{} and unroll it over a #9-inch pie plate{}, metal if you have one. Ease it into the plate without stretching.

Trim to 1/2 inch past the rim, fold the overhang under so it sits flush with the edge, press to seal and flute. Wrap and chill at least ~{1%hour}, or up to 24 hours.

Heat the oven to 350°F (175°C) with a rack in the middle. Set the shell on a #baking sheet{}, line it with two layers of parchment and fill to the top with pie weights.

Bake ~{15%minutes} to 20 minutes, until the edge is light golden. Lift out the weights, turn the plate and bake ~{15%minutes} to 20 minutes more, until the bottom is dry and light golden. Prick any bubbles with a knife tip. It need not cool before filling.

== Filling ==

In a #10-inch skillet{}, cook the @bacon{8%oz}(cut into 1/2-inch pieces) over medium heat, stirring often, ~{7%minutes} to 9 minutes, until browned and crisp. Lift it onto paper towels. Keep 1 tbsp of the fat in the pan and pour off the rest.

Heat the fat until shimmering. Cook the @shallots{2}(finely chopped) ~{3%minutes} to 4 minutes, until soft and browned. Add the @garlic{2%cloves}(minced) and @fresh thyme{1%tsp}(minced) for ~{30%seconds}, then take off the heat and cool ~{10%minutes}.

In a #large bowl{}, whisk the @eggs{4} until no streaks of white remain. Whisk in the @lemon zest{1%tsp}(from 1 lemon), @salt{1/2%tsp}, @black pepper{1/2%tsp} and @?nutmeg{1%pinch}(freshly grated), then the @whole milk{3/4%cup}, @heavy cream{3/4%cup} and the shallots.

Scatter half the @Gruyère{4%oz}(grated, about 1 cup), half the @Parmesan{1%oz}(grated, about 1/4 cup) and half the bacon over the crust. Whisk the custard again, pour it in slowly, and top with the rest of the cheese and bacon.

Bake on the sheet at 350°F (175°C) ~{40%minutes} to 50 minutes, turning halfway, until lightly browned and a knife 1 inch from the edge comes out clean. The middle should still wobble slightly and read 170°F (75°C).

Cool on a #wire rack{} ~{2%hours} to 3 hours, until just warm, before slicing.
